WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.377
This is the executive briefing
for Book 12 of the Federal

3
00:00:04.377 --> 00:00:08.299
Modernization Workbook, Security
Operations and Telemetry.

4
00:00:08.299 --> 00:00:13.775
It's for the CISO, the SOC lead,
the CIO and authorizing officials.

5
00:00:13.775 --> 00:00:15.200
The argument is short.

6
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.620
You can't defend what you can't see, and
the gaps that matter most make no noise:

7
00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:24.325
a system that never sent its logs looks
exactly like one where nothing happened.

8
00:00:24.325 --> 00:00:27.600
Storing logs isn't the same
as detecting with them.

9
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.488
And a finding closes only when
the thing is fixed, with proof.

10
00:00:31.488 --> 00:00:34.725
I'll take four questions:
why, what, who and how.

11
00:00:34.725 --> 00:00:39.100
Each decision slide names the decision,
its owner and when it's needed.

12
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.960
Let's start with what a
dashboard can't show you.

13
00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:46.422
Most agencies can show a dashboard
with millions of events a day.

14
00:00:46.422 --> 00:00:48.448
But that only measures what arrived.

15
00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:49.847
It can't show what didn't.

16
00:00:49.847 --> 00:00:53.752
Gaps stay silent in four ways: a
source that was never connected,

17
00:00:53.752 --> 00:00:58.182
a connector that looks healthy but
delivers nothing, data stored where

18
00:00:58.182 --> 00:01:02.536
no rule ever reads it, and logging
that someone switched off on purpose.

19
00:01:02.536 --> 00:01:06.258
The 2023 intrusion into
government mailboxes was caught

20
00:01:06.258 --> 00:01:09.360
by an agency that happened
to have the right logs.

21
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.610
Others had nothing to look
at, and nothing told them so.

22
00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:16.285
So the takeaway: measure coverage
against your asset inventory,

23
00:01:16.285 --> 00:01:17.810
not against what showed up.

24
00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.600
Next, the difference between
storing and detecting.

25
00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.294
There's a difference that's
easy to buy as one thing:

26
00:01:25.294 --> 00:01:29.387
a place where logs are kept, and a
capability that actually reads them.

27
00:01:29.387 --> 00:01:31.200
The controls keep them apart.

28
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.638
Generating records is one requirement;
reviewing and analyzing them is another.

29
00:01:36.638 --> 00:01:40.599
And the new federal logging memo
scores maturity by how much of the

30
00:01:40.599 --> 00:01:44.488
required activity produces real
alerts, not by how much you store.

31
00:01:44.488 --> 00:01:48.926
Data kept only in a cheap storage
tier raises no alerts at all.

32
00:01:48.926 --> 00:01:53.561
So the decision for the CISO is a simple
rule: a source counts as monitored

33
00:01:53.561 --> 00:01:58.425
only when an enabled rule reads it, and
its output reaches a queue someone owns.

34
00:01:58.425 --> 00:02:01.380
Next, the federal requirement itself.

35
00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:11.730
In May 2026, OMB memo M-26-14 rescinded
M-21-31, effective immediately.

36
00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:16.405
So the retention numbers many teams
still quote belong to a memo that's gone.

37
00:02:16.405 --> 00:02:20.222
The new floor is simpler: logs
searchable for six months,

38
00:02:20.222 --> 00:02:24.205
retrievable for a year, and
available to your top-level SOC.

39
00:02:24.205 --> 00:02:28.143
The clock runs from CISA's
architecture, published in August.

40
00:02:28.143 --> 00:02:31.815
By our count, your logging
plan is due November 18,

41
00:02:31.815 --> 00:02:35.580
and the maturity levels
follow through July 2027.

42
00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:39.030
Those dates are our arithmetic, not OMB's.

43
00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:42.287
The decision: the CISO
owns that logging plan,

44
00:02:42.287 --> 00:02:46.743
and the CIO funds it as a visibility
plan, not a storage purchase.

45
00:02:46.743 --> 00:02:49.680
Next, how the evidence fits together.

46
00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:54.992
Every management tool reports in its
own format against its own inventory.

47
00:02:54.992 --> 00:02:58.563
Five tools, five inventories,
and when an assessor asks

48
00:02:58.563 --> 00:03:01.817
you to prove a fix across
the whole estate, you can't.

49
00:03:01.817 --> 00:03:06.436
The answer is an evidence contract:
every source emits the same record,

50
00:03:06.436 --> 00:03:11.531
joined on one authoritative identity
for each asset, plus synchronized time.

51
00:03:11.531 --> 00:03:14.151
That identity has to come from one place,

52
00:03:14.151 --> 00:03:16.930
the naming plane, not
from each tool's own ID.

53
00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:21.561
So the decision: stand up the naming plane
before you build the evidence pipeline,

54
00:03:21.561 --> 00:03:24.118
and write the contract
into the logging plan.

55
00:03:24.118 --> 00:03:29.604
The CISO owns the plan; the DNS and
identity teams own the naming plane.

56
00:03:29.604 --> 00:03:32.270
Next, where telemetry comes from.

57
00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:38.095
Telemetry isn't collected; it's emitted,
and the switch lives in each platform.

58
00:03:38.095 --> 00:03:42.644
So the estate is six planes:
identity, cloud platform,

59
00:03:42.644 --> 00:03:47.933
Microsoft 365, endpoints, network
and data, each with an owner.

60
00:03:47.933 --> 00:03:52.470
No single plane tells the whole story,
and contractor-run systems count too.

61
00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:53.807
Watch the defaults.

62
00:03:53.807 --> 00:03:59.360
Several native logs quietly expire after
a month or three unless you export them,

63
00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:03.670
and Microsoft 365 audit history
depends on each user's license.

64
00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:05.808
So the decision splits on purpose.

65
00:04:05.808 --> 00:04:10.107
The CISO decides what each plane
must emit, in the logging plan.

66
00:04:10.107 --> 00:04:13.783
Each platform owner decides
how, and keeps it switched on.

67
00:04:13.783 --> 00:04:17.070
Next, keeping it on
while everything changes.

68
00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.570
Modernization is a long run of changes,

69
00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:24.008
and change is exactly when
telemetry goes quiet.

70
00:04:24.008 --> 00:04:28.645
New resources can be born silent
unless policy forces them to emit.

71
00:04:28.645 --> 00:04:31.770
Older ones only get fixed when
someone runs the cleanup.

72
00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:35.183
Retired resources leave
stale settings behind.

73
00:04:35.183 --> 00:04:40.295
And the security console itself is
moving to a new portal by March 2027.

74
00:04:40.295 --> 00:04:43.032
The lesson is to check flow, not settings.

75
00:04:43.032 --> 00:04:46.795
A setting that saved without error
doesn't prove anything arrived.

76
00:04:46.795 --> 00:04:51.767
So the decision: platform owners keep
every plane emitting through each change,

77
00:04:51.767 --> 00:04:54.705
security engineering
checks freshness daily,

78
00:04:54.705 --> 00:04:58.245
and an unexplained loss of logs
is treated as an incident.

79
00:04:58.245 --> 00:05:00.410
Next, the SIEM decision.

80
00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:05.610
The SIEM decision is less about which
product and more about how it behaves.

81
00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.964
A log store on its own is a
warehouse; scheduled rules,

82
00:05:08.964 --> 00:05:12.397
incidents and automation
make it a detection engine.

83
00:05:12.397 --> 00:05:14.347
The program's split is design.

84
00:05:14.347 --> 00:05:18.811
Your endpoint and identity protection
tools detect what their own sensors see.

85
00:05:18.811 --> 00:05:22.214
The SIEM covers everything
they can't, like DNS,

86
00:05:22.214 --> 00:05:25.710
network services,
databases and other clouds.

87
00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:29.213
And you only stream raw data
where a rule actually needs it,

88
00:05:29.213 --> 00:05:31.110
because streaming costs money.

89
00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:35.097
Check each feature in your own government
cloud before you design on it.

90
00:05:35.097 --> 00:05:39.110
Security engineering sets that
division, with one incident queue.

91
00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:41.420
Next, how long to keep it all.

92
00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:44.757
Retention is really two decisions.

93
00:05:44.757 --> 00:05:46.746
First, the security floor.

94
00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:51.370
Data your rules depend on stays in the
alerting tier for at least six months,

95
00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:53.683
because only that tier raises alerts.

96
00:05:53.683 --> 00:05:58.091
Older or high-volume data can live in
cheaper tiers you can still search,

97
00:05:58.091 --> 00:06:00.707
and a tamper-proof copy
serves as evidence.

98
00:06:00.707 --> 00:06:06.019
Second, disposition: how long beyond the
floor, and when data can be destroyed.

99
00:06:06.019 --> 00:06:10.220
That belongs to the records officer,
not to whoever holds the console.

100
00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:11.843
So the CISO sets the floor,

101
00:06:11.843 --> 00:06:16.123
and nobody shortens retention
without the records officer agreeing,

102
00:06:16.123 --> 00:06:20.845
because once logs are gone, an investigation
or a legal hold can't get them back.

103
00:06:20.845 --> 00:06:22.810
Next, the cost side.

104
00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:28.410
Here's the uncomfortable truth: most
visibility gaps are decided in the budget.

105
00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:32.660
Cheap storage tiers are a sound
choice for noisy, low-value data.

106
00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:36.778
The problem comes when a source that
matters is moved there to save money,

107
00:06:36.778 --> 00:06:39.660
and nobody records that it
no longer raises alerts.

108
00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:42.764
Streaming every raw data
feed costs money too,

109
00:06:42.764 --> 00:06:45.460
and audit history depends on licensing.

110
00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:47.385
So the decision has two halves.

111
00:06:47.385 --> 00:06:53.285
The CIO funds ingestion and retention as
a visibility decision, not a storage line.

112
00:06:53.285 --> 00:06:56.756
And when a detection source
does leave the alerting tier,

113
00:06:56.756 --> 00:07:01.335
that's recorded as a gap, which the
authorizing official accepts or rejects.

114
00:07:01.335 --> 00:07:03.910
Next, what happens to findings.

115
00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:07.285
Detection is only one kind of finding.

116
00:07:07.285 --> 00:07:11.144
A missing patch, a known
exploited flaw, a drifted setting,

117
00:07:11.144 --> 00:07:15.085
an exposed storage account: they
all go into the same queue.

118
00:07:15.085 --> 00:07:16.710
And the clock changed this year.

119
00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:19.337
CISA's directive BOD 26-04, from June,

120
00:07:19.337 --> 00:07:24.592
replaced the old rules with four
questions about each vulnerable asset:

121
00:07:24.592 --> 00:07:27.919
is it exposed, is the flaw
known to be exploited,

122
00:07:27.919 --> 00:07:32.298
can the attack be automated, and
how much control does it give.

123
00:07:32.298 --> 00:07:36.198
The answers set deadlines from
three days up to the next upgrade.

124
00:07:36.198 --> 00:07:39.434
Remember, a scanner
measures; it never fixes.

125
00:07:39.434 --> 00:07:41.904
The system owner remediates on that clock,

126
00:07:41.904 --> 00:07:45.148
and a missed deadline goes
to the authorizing official.

127
00:07:45.148 --> 00:07:47.150
Now, who runs all this.

128
00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:49.987
Most agencies can name their SOC.

129
00:07:49.987 --> 00:07:54.008
Fewer can say who decided what gets
collected, how long it's kept,

130
00:07:54.008 --> 00:07:57.100
or who accepted the risk
when a source went quiet.

131
00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.300
Usually it's whoever held the console.

132
00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:01.813
The book's model
separates those decisions.

133
00:08:01.813 --> 00:08:03.663
The SOC runs the queue.

134
00:08:03.663 --> 00:08:06.661
The CIO funds it, the
CISO sets requirements,

135
00:08:06.661 --> 00:08:12.174
platform owners keep logs flowing, and
the authorizing official accepts the risk.

136
00:08:12.174 --> 00:08:16.250
And because every system inherits
monitoring from the SOC,

137
00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:20.325
the SOC's own controls need to
be assessed like anyone else's.

138
00:08:20.325 --> 00:08:23.250
The first decision belongs to the CIO:

139
00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:28.062
designate one top-level SOC, decide
how it's staffed, and fund it.

140
00:08:28.062 --> 00:08:30.320
Next, the full matrix.

141
00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:33.008
Here's the matrix in one view.

142
00:08:33.008 --> 00:08:35.546
The CIO decides scope and funding.

143
00:08:35.546 --> 00:08:39.312
The CISO decides the logging
plan, what each plane emits,

144
00:08:39.312 --> 00:08:43.320
the retention floor, and any
automated action on production.

145
00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:46.208
The SOC decides triage and closure.

146
00:08:46.208 --> 00:08:49.794
Security engineering owns
each detection rule,

147
00:08:49.794 --> 00:08:54.008
and every rule has one named
owner, a test and a review date.

148
00:08:54.008 --> 00:08:56.583
The records officer decides disposition.

149
00:08:56.583 --> 00:08:59.444
And the authorizing
official decides the risks.

150
00:08:59.444 --> 00:09:01.335
One row has a federal clock:

151
00:09:01.335 --> 00:09:06.232
the SOC must notify CISA within one
hour of identifying an incident.

152
00:09:06.232 --> 00:09:09.285
There's no time to send
that decision up and back,

153
00:09:09.285 --> 00:09:13.120
so the SOC owns it, while the
CISO decides whether it's major.

154
00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:16.200
Next, the risks nobody signs for.

155
00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:19.638
Only the authorizing
official can accept risk,

156
00:09:19.638 --> 00:09:21.913
and that includes the risk of not seeing.

157
00:09:21.913 --> 00:09:25.901
Four risks tend to get accepted
silently by whoever is closest:

158
00:09:25.901 --> 00:09:30.350
a source that isn't collected, a noisy
rule switched off with no end date,

159
00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:34.338
retention that's too short, and a
fix that will miss its deadline.

160
00:09:34.338 --> 00:09:36.125
Each one should be written up,

161
00:09:36.125 --> 00:09:39.400
recommended by the CISO, and
decided with an end date.

162
00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:42.462
And security operations
keeps its own roles apart.

163
00:09:42.462 --> 00:09:45.156
A contractor can prepare
a risk acceptance,

164
00:09:45.156 --> 00:09:47.850
but only the authorizing
official signs it.

165
00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:51.348
The rule: an exception with
an end date is the CISO's;

166
00:09:51.348 --> 00:09:54.512
one without goes to the
authorizing official.

167
00:09:54.512 --> 00:09:56.300
Now, how to respond.

168
00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:59.425
A rule nobody owns slowly rots.

169
00:09:59.425 --> 00:10:02.493
So the program keeps its
detection rules as code,

170
00:10:02.493 --> 00:10:06.175
under version control, and nobody
edits a live rule by hand.

171
00:10:06.175 --> 00:10:09.877
Every rule follows the same
path: tested against history,

172
00:10:09.877 --> 00:10:13.895
run quietly in alert-only mode
while false alarms are counted,

173
00:10:13.895 --> 00:10:16.258
promoted once its response is wired,

174
00:10:16.258 --> 00:10:21.300
then reviewed on a schedule to check it
still fires and its data still arrives.

175
00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:22.950
Be honest about the limits.

176
00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:26.069
Detection takes minutes
to hours, not seconds,

177
00:10:26.069 --> 00:10:29.187
and without steady upkeep
the library decays.

178
00:10:29.187 --> 00:10:33.925
The decision: security engineering runs
the library this way, with the CISO

179
00:10:33.925 --> 00:10:38.738
setting priorities, and no rule opens
incidents before it has passed through.

180
00:10:38.738 --> 00:10:40.620
Next, response.

181
00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:44.732
Detection without response
doesn't reduce your exposure.

182
00:10:44.732 --> 00:10:49.295
But automation without a gate can
turn one noisy rule into an outage.

183
00:10:49.295 --> 00:10:51.045
So responses climb a ladder.

184
00:10:51.045 --> 00:10:52.958
Every alert is recorded.

185
00:10:52.958 --> 00:10:56.457
Medium confidence gets context
and an analyst decides.

186
00:10:56.457 --> 00:11:00.744
High confidence gets a prepared
containment, released by a named approver.

187
00:11:00.744 --> 00:11:05.208
Fully automatic action is only for
a short list of low-risk cases.

188
00:11:05.208 --> 00:11:09.434
Three things always keep a person
in the loop: isolating a server,

189
00:11:09.434 --> 00:11:14.219
disabling a privileged or emergency
account, and any tenant-wide change.

190
00:11:14.219 --> 00:11:18.796
The decision: the CISO approves every
playbook that acts on production, and the

191
00:11:18.796 --> 00:11:21.291
authorizing official decides whether the

192
00:11:21.291 --> 00:11:23.995
vendor's own automatic
containment is allowed.

193
00:11:23.995 --> 00:11:25.730
Next, evidence.

194
00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:30.217
Continuous monitoring isn't a
claim that monitoring happens.

195
00:11:30.217 --> 00:11:33.180
It's evidence an assessor
can check, and run again.

196
00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:37.530
The practical test is simple: show me
an alert from the last thirty days,

197
00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:40.455
the incident it opened
and what you did about it.

198
00:11:40.455 --> 00:11:44.338
So every incident keeps four
parts: the alert, the ticket,

199
00:11:44.338 --> 00:11:47.630
the response notes, and
a closure with its proof.

200
00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:50.018
Closing a finding takes three steps.

201
00:11:50.018 --> 00:11:51.829
The platform owner fixes it.

202
00:11:51.829 --> 00:11:54.142
The next scan or check shows it's gone.

203
00:11:54.142 --> 00:11:55.817
And the record links the two.

204
00:11:55.817 --> 00:12:01.205
The decision: a finding closes only on
that re-test, never on a ticket status.

205
00:12:01.205 --> 00:12:04.750
Next, how you know the
monitoring itself is working.

206
00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:07.994
You need a small set of
metrics that tell you

207
00:12:07.994 --> 00:12:10.487
whether monitoring works,
not how busy it is.

208
00:12:10.487 --> 00:12:14.887
Coverage counts the required activities
a working rule actually reads,

209
00:12:14.887 --> 00:12:16.500
not how many rules you have.

210
00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:20.175
Completeness counts how long a
required source stayed silent.

211
00:12:20.175 --> 00:12:25.325
Vulnerability remediation counts fixes
closed on time, and fixes overdue.

212
00:12:25.325 --> 00:12:29.533
Configuration counts passes,
failures and exceptions separately,

213
00:12:29.533 --> 00:12:31.937
so an exception never looks like a pass.

214
00:12:31.937 --> 00:12:35.876
And maturity is reported by
system, at its weakest point.

215
00:12:35.876 --> 00:12:40.483
The decision belongs to the CISO:
adopt the metric set and the rhythm,

216
00:12:40.483 --> 00:12:45.575
with the SOC seeing it daily, and the
CISO and authorizing official monthly.

217
00:12:45.575 --> 00:12:47.800
Let's pull the decisions together.

218
00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:50.125
So, the decisions.

219
00:12:50.125 --> 00:12:55.145
The CISO owns the logging plan due November
18, decides what each plane emits,

220
00:12:55.145 --> 00:12:58.597
counts a source as monitored
only when a rule reads it,

221
00:12:58.597 --> 00:13:01.813
and approves any automated
action on production.

222
00:13:01.813 --> 00:13:06.933
The CIO funds visibility, not storage,
and designates one top-level SOC.

223
00:13:06.933 --> 00:13:11.525
Platform owners keep their logs
flowing through every change.

224
00:13:11.525 --> 00:13:15.188
Security engineering runs the
detection library as code.

225
00:13:15.188 --> 00:13:18.663
The records officer must agree
before retention shrinks.

226
00:13:18.663 --> 00:13:21.563
The SOC notifies CISA within the hour.

227
00:13:21.563 --> 00:13:26.337
And the authorizing official decides
every open-ended gap and missed deadline.

228
00:13:26.337 --> 00:13:29.000
A gap nobody chose is still a decision.

229
00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:30.275
Make it on purpose.

230
00:13:30.275 --> 00:13:33.963
Book 12 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

231
00:13:33.963 --> 00:13:34.800
Thank you.
